--- body ---
## Context

Ruled 2026-09-07 (`DECISIONS.md`): R34 is satisfied when a reply returns the
right work and a page intersecting the target's page range, and two scores are
tracked, of which only the one reading the system's own reported page is
official.

Measured against the golden run's own artifact before the ruling was written:
of 1 928 hits across 843 replies, **none carries a page**. The reply schema has
the field. The engine returns item key, title, snippet and score, so nothing
fills it.

So the official score is zero today, and stays zero until this gap closes. That
is deliberate — it keeps the gap visible in every run rather than buried in a
ticket — but the gap itself is not a scoring problem. It is R24 unmet:

> **R24. Locator.** A hit MUST lead to the page it came from, and one entry
> MUST give one hit.

A hit that hands back a snippet with no page has not led the reader anywhere.
This ticket is that clause measured, not a new demand.

## What is needed

A retrieval reply must carry, per hit, the page the evidence came from, and say
what kind of page it is: the printed folio where the extraction knows it, an
estimate where it is derived, per D10. Where the passage straddles a boundary
the reply gives the range rather than one number, which is what makes the
ruled intersection test computable on both sides.

The extraction already knows this. Zotero's own full-text cache marks page
breaks with a form feed, which is how the accommodating score derives a page
offline. What is missing is the passage-to-page mapping surviving into the
index and out through the query path.

## Actions

1. Establish where the page is lost: whether the passage record keeps a page
   or an offset at all, and if not, at which stage it is dropped.
2. State what it costs to carry it: the index column, the write path, and
   whether an existing index can gain it without a rebuild (R23, R3).
3. Propose the reply-shape change, and the estimate flag D10 requires.
4. Prepare it as an upstream contribution rather than a fork-local patch: this
   is a promise the shipped product makes, not an artifact of the bench.

## Where the page is lost (established from source, 2026-09-07)

Read in the `fork/` checkout at the repo root, at the reviewed baseline named
by `SYNC.md` (the upstream v1.14.0 line). Every line cited below was read, not
inferred.

**Two passage paths exist in the upstream tree, and only one carries pages.**

*The full-text passage path — already correct, and not the one the bench
queries.* `fork/src/features/search/chunker.ts:41` `chunkWithOffsets()` chunks
the RAW text and returns `{index, text, start, end}` char offsets.
`fork/src/features/fulltext/passages.ts:5-15` defines `Passage` with
`charStart`, `charEnd`, `section`, `pageApprox` (its own comment: "proportional
page estimate") and `page` ("exact page from PDF re-extraction").
`passages.ts:18-22` `approxPage()` derives the estimate;
`fork/src/features/fulltext/pdf-pages.ts:127` `locatePage()` derives the exact
page from a re-extraction. This is the `get_fulltext` / W1 `precise_pages`
path.

*The search-index path — where the page is lost.* Three distinct losses, in
order along the path:

1. **`fork/src/features/search/chunker.ts:8`** — `chunkText()` opens with
   `text.replace(/\s+/g, ' ')`. The form feed that Zotero's full-text cache
   uses to mark a page break is whitespace, so every page boundary is destroyed
   in the first statement of the function, before any chunk exists. `Chunk`
   (`chunker.ts:1-4`) is `{index, text}` — no offset either, so the page cannot
   be recovered downstream by arithmetic. This is the earliest point at which
   the information is already gone.
2. **`fork/src/features/search/backend.ts:61-68`** — `ChunkRecord` is
   `{id, itemKey, title, text, source?}`. No page, and no offset field that
   could hold one. It is written at
   `fork/src/features/search/index-manager.ts:2086-2098` (`addFulltext`, the
   attachment-body path a full-text hit comes from) and again at `2111`
   (`addOwnWords`).
3. **`fork/src/features/search/sqlite-index.ts:930-937`** — the `passages`
   table DDL is `(pid, id, item_key, title, text, source, vector)`. **No page
   column, no offset column.** So even a fixed chunker has nowhere to persist
   one in an existing index.

**The reply shape, and why the harness sees no page.**
`fork/src/features/search/backend.ts:51-58` — `SearchHit` is
`{itemKey, title, snippet, score, source?}`. Those are exactly the four fields
`DECISIONS.md` records the golden run as receiving. The reply schema's page
field is never populated because the type the engine returns has no page member
to populate it from. On the scoring side, `bench/generator/score.py` reads a
hit's page under `CHAIN_FIELDS["page_label"] = ("pageLabel", "page_label",
"page", "printedPage")` — none of which `SearchHit` can ever carry.

Corroborated by the committed golden artifact
`bench/results/0719-generator/run.json`, whose `chain_in_reply.carried` reads
`entry_title: 26` against `answer_rows_found: 26`, and `page_label: 0`,
`section: 0`, `identifier: 0`, `date: 0`, `entry_creators: 0`.

**Ruled out.** The loss is not in the extraction — the same `run.json` records
`identity.extraction.text_source` as "Zotero's own extraction, over the local
API's /fulltext", which is the export that carries the form feeds. It is not in
the query or fusion path, and not in the tool layer: it is upstream of all
three, in the chunker's first statement and in a schema with nowhere to put a
page.

## The cost and the reply shape (2026-09-07)

Criteria 2 and 3 are answered in
[`conception/0734-carrying-the-page-into-the-reply.md`](../conception/0734-carrying-the-page-into-the-reply.md),
written as the reviewable upstream artifact criterion 4's action 4 asks for.
The verdicts, so the ticket carries its own record:

- **A fourth loss, upstream of the three recorded above.**
  `fork/src/features/search/fulltext-source.ts:167-193` joins several
  attachments' texts into one string and keeps only `ft.content`, discarding the
  attachment key and the `indexedPages` / `totalPages` the router already
  returned (`fork/src/api/web-client.ts:282`). The interface forbids carrying
  them: `textFor` is `(itemKey) => Promise<string | undefined>`
  (`fulltext-source.ts:26`), as is `fulltextFor` (`backend.ts:425`).
- **Cost, criterion 2. No rebuild is implied by the schema; a full re-chunk is
  implied by the chunker.** The migration ladder is dispatched on a `meta` row
  read before any write (`sqlite-index.ts:528-547`, `585-645`, `655-667`,
  `680-702`) and holds one rung, `to: 2` (`175-201`). A page can be added
  without touching the stamp at all, following the `vector_codes` precedent the
  file argues for in its own words (`53-61`, and the row-layout argument at
  `946-949`): a side table created by `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS`, which every
  older build ignores because every statement names its columns. That satisfies
  **R23** in both directions, where a stamp bump satisfies it only forwards and
  sidelines every existing index for an older build.
- **A backfill has no source, and that is the finding.** `chunkText` collapses
  whitespace at `chunker.ts:8` and slices the collapsed string at `21`; nothing
  stored recovers the raw position. A rung cannot go and look either:
  `SchemaMigration.up` is `up(db: Database): void` (`sqlite-index.ts:165`),
  synchronous, its only argument the database handle, called inside
  `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`. A rung can add the column and cannot fill it. Values arrive
  only when the rows are written again.
- **R3 is what forces the vector salvage.** A re-chunk is proportional to what
  changed under C1 (SPEC.md:751-753) and is R3-conformant; a whole-library
  re-embed is not, since the vector's inputs did not move. `adoptVector`
  (`sqlite-index.ts:881-898`) already reuses a vector when id and text match byte
  for byte, so a chunker whose output text is unchanged keeps every vector. But
  the salvage is armed only in `priceOf` (`770-800`), reached only from
  `sideline()`, so `action:"refresh"` today pays the full re-embed the file
  prices at five and a half hours of local CPU (`53-61`, `143-146`).
- **Reply shape, criterion 3.** `SearchHit` gains `attachmentKey?` and exactly
  one of `page?: string` (printed folio or folio range, exact) or
  `pageApprox?: string` (derived). The names and the presence-is-the-label rule
  are reused verbatim from `fork/src/features/fulltext/passages.ts:5-15` rather
  than invented; the one deviation is the type, `string` instead of `number`,
  because a straddling passage needs a range and the gate's `page_set`
  (`bench/golden_gate.py:269-289`) reads `"12-13"` as `{12, 13}`.
- **`page` is not fillable today.** No page-label reader exists anywhere in
  `fork/src`; `annotate.ts:225` shows the product's convention is
  `String(pageIndex + 1)`, an ordinal used as a label. `pdfjs-dist` does expose
  `getPageLabels()` (`fork/node_modules/pdfjs-dist/types/src/display/api.d.ts:945`)
  but only on the PDF-file path the index build never takes.

## What criterion 4 would cost (stated, not run)

Nothing was built. `make golden-run` needs `fork/dist/index.js`
(`Makefile:371`, `388`), so it implies `make upstream-checkout` then
`npm ci && npm run build` in `fork/`, plus an empty `GOLDEN_DATA_DIR`
(`389`). Six fork files change, plus one harness edit in this repo:
`bench/golden_run.py:172` does not read `pageApprox`.

**Measured ceiling**, over the committed bank and export with the gate's own
`page_set` and `Export.page_span` as the instrument: if the engine reported the
extraction's form-feed ordinal perfectly, R34 official tops out at **15/195**;
if it reported the printed folio perfectly, at **85/195**. 110 of the 195 scored
questions carry no printed page on any alternate, and `official_page_verdict`
(`bench/golden_gate.py:1066-1072`) returns unsatisfied on an empty target
whatever the reply says. The ordinal is not the folio: of the 108 alternates
carrying both a folio and a locatable offset, 18 agree and 90 differ, by
per-work offsets from +1 to +28 and one of −100, with 30 folios not arabic at
all. So closing this ticket as scoped moves the official reading off zero and
moves it a little; it does not make the red-state exercise's nine arms
distinguishable on the official score.

## Open for the author (recorded, not answered)

1. Is a ceiling of 85/195 the intended shape of the official reading, or should
   it be scored over the questions that carry a printed page?
2. Does a labelled estimate satisfy R34's official reading? If yes,
   `golden_run.py:172` must read `pageApprox`; if no, the official reading
   cannot move until the product gains a printed-folio source.
3. D10 and `passages.ts` are two-valued over three sources (printed folio,
   extraction ordinal, proportional estimate). The note maps the latter two onto
   `pageApprox`. Confirm, or split the vocabulary.

## Still to do

- **Exit criterion 4 — nothing measured.** No build was run in either session.
- **Nothing was implemented in `fork/`.** Note for whoever does: a
  worktree-isolated agent cannot run version-control commands inside `fork/`,
  because the isolation guard refuses any redirect into the shared checkout.
  Reading it by absolute path works.

## Exit criteria
- [x] where the page is lost between extraction and reply is established from
      source, not inferred
- [x] the cost of carrying it stated, including whether a rebuild is implied
- [x] a reply shape proposed that gives a page or a page range per hit, with
      the estimate flag D10 requires
- [ ] R34's official score becomes measurable, and its first non-zero reading
      is recorded
